1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,040
May 1940.

2
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:13,620
The world watches hopelessly as Nazi invaders

3
00:00:13,621 --> 00:00:16,080
mount a blitzkrieg attack against France

4
00:00:16,081 --> 00:00:21,400
and march triumphantly through the streets of Paris.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
But behind the tears of a defeated people comes the rumbling of rebellion.

6
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:44,000
Enraged French citizens answer General de Gaulle's command and fight for freedom

7
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:49,000
with an underground organization committed to sabotage and other acts of defiance.

8
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:56,000
This resistance grew bit by bit, comprised of both men and women,

9
00:00:56,001 --> 00:01:03,000
and women, regardless of political, moral, or religious persuasion.

10
00:01:03,001 --> 00:01:08,520
I became a founder of the Parisian Liberation Committee and the organization that directed

11
00:01:08,521 --> 00:01:12,460
the resistance and the national insurrection.

12
00:01:12,461 --> 00:01:17,600
Hitler himself visited France during the occupation.

13
00:01:17,601 --> 00:01:19,400
He marveled at the great monuments.

14
00:01:19,401 --> 00:01:24,960
He reveled in his conquest.

15
00:01:24,961 --> 00:01:30,640
Then the Eiffel Tower, 51 years old and over a thousand feet tall, became a hostage as

16
00:01:30,641 --> 00:01:36,000
Germans exploited its great height, using the tower as an antenna to send coded radio

17
00:01:36,001 --> 00:01:38,960
signals to their armies.

18
00:01:38,961 --> 00:01:45,400
Ironically, the silhouette of the tower against the Paris skyline offered hope to the free

19
00:01:45,401 --> 00:01:47,820
French underground.

20
00:01:47,821 --> 00:01:53,020
It was a symbol of liberty, a symbol of France.

21
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:57,940
Under the occupation, the idea predominated among all the French

22
00:01:57,941 --> 00:02:01,860
to know that one day one would be free to climb up the Eiffel Tower.

23
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:05,900
When Allied troops finally liberated Paris,

24
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:10,340
this handful of free Frenchmen celebrated by climbing the Eiffel Tower

25
00:02:10,341 --> 00:02:13,180
and unfurling the French tricolor flag.

26
00:02:13,181 --> 00:02:18,180
Down below, citizens spat and cursed at the fleeing Germans.

27
00:02:18,181 --> 00:02:23,180
But from the pinnacle of the tower, liberty, equality, and fraternity

28
00:02:23,181 --> 00:02:26,180
reclaimed its mantle of glory.

29
00:02:26,181 --> 00:02:30,180
For most of the world, the Eiffel Tower is France.

30
00:02:30,181 --> 00:02:33,180
It is light and hope, poetry and magic.

31
00:02:33,181 --> 00:02:37,180
Her complex geometry holds the dreams of lovers.

32
00:02:37,181 --> 00:02:40,180
The French may enjoy the distinct honor of having created

33
00:02:40,181 --> 00:02:42,180
and nurtured this international monument,

34
00:02:42,181 --> 00:02:46,180
but her home is in the hearts of free people everywhere.

35
00:02:46,181 --> 00:02:52,180
Tonight on Modern Marvels, La Tour Eiffel, the Eiffel Tower.

36
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:26,180
There is something about great height that suggests a spirit of triumph to the soul.

37
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:31,000
It forces people's vision to heaven,

38
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:35,200
while it offers a calming perspective to the chaos of the world below.

39
00:03:35,201 --> 00:03:42,520
throughout the ages people have challenged architectural and engineering limits to

40
00:03:42,521 --> 00:03:49,660
achieve great structural height sometimes to spy on enemies approaching from a distance

41
00:03:49,661 --> 00:03:57,580
and at other times in an attempt to get closer to God there are those who insist on finding

42
00:03:57,581 --> 00:04:04,300
practical applications for towers they make good broadcast antennas or they're a fine

43
00:04:04,301 --> 00:04:06,910
place for a revolving restaurant.

44
00:04:06,911 --> 00:04:11,340
But the real appeal of towers comes from a purer notion.

45
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:17,980
Towers are a celebration of will. They speak to our ambitions.

46
00:04:17,981 --> 00:04:20,300
They are the joyous declaration

47
00:04:20,301 --> 00:04:28,489
that we exist. The world's best loved tower is the Eiffel Tower in Paris.

48
00:04:28,490 --> 00:04:30,620
Its soaring height,

49
00:04:30,621 --> 00:04:33,880
its delicate lines, its airy volume,

50
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:38,820
all reflect the best part of the French spirit.

51
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:43,760
Designed and built in 1889,

52
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:46,260
it was the centerpiece in a celebration

53
00:04:46,261 --> 00:04:49,640
of the bloody French Revolution 100 years earlier.

54
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,880
On July 14, 1789,

55
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:56,040
in a flood of revolutionary passion,

56
00:04:56,041 --> 00:04:59,920
Parisian artisans led an attack against French aristocracy

57
00:04:59,921 --> 00:05:01,920
and stormed the Bastille,

58
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:04,080
the infamous prison where the poor

59
00:05:04,081 --> 00:05:06,680
and politically incorrect were locked away.

60
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:13,240
The insurrection spread across France

61
00:05:13,241 --> 00:05:16,720
and patriots rallied to the battle cry of freedom,

62
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:19,580
the Marseillaise.

63
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:23,860
Let us go, children of the fatherland.

64
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:26,460
Our day of glory has arrived.

65
00:05:26,461 --> 00:05:31,100
Against us stands tyranny, the bloody flag is raised.

66
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:35,580
Come together in the countryside to lower these savage soldiers.

67
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:41,300
They come right into our arms to cut the throats of your sons, your country.

68
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:46,220
To arm citizens, form your battalions, let us march,

69
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:49,720
that their impure blood should water our fields.

70
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:55,680
This nationalistic eruption reverberated across Europe

71
00:05:55,681 --> 00:05:59,360
and set the stage for modern democratic movements.

72
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:06,300
And the French people were justifiably proud of the positive contributions their revolution had made.

73
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:13,200
But a century later, the French ego was bruised after a devastating military defeat to Germany.

74
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:17,680
The idea of mounting a spectacular international exposition

75
00:06:17,681 --> 00:06:23,900
seemed like a grand way to remember past glory and forget about contemporary setbacks.

76
00:06:23,901 --> 00:06:37,000
They were still sad because there was in 1870 a terrible war with Germany and we had lost two provinces, Alsace and Lorraine.

77
00:06:37,001 --> 00:06:44,900
And for that, French people were very pleased to have this exhibition to show they were very great.

78
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:48,380
The country was quite rich.

79
00:06:48,381 --> 00:06:52,380
It was decided there needed to be a centerpiece for the celebration,

80
00:06:52,381 --> 00:06:56,380
an artistic statement which would loom over the crowds.

81
00:06:56,381 --> 00:07:03,380
The bravado of the industrial age suggested an engineering accomplishment, a tower.

82
00:07:03,381 --> 00:07:11,380
The tower was important as something very clever in technology.

83
00:07:11,381 --> 00:07:18,380
nobody in the world was able to build a tower of a thousand feet.

84
00:07:18,381 --> 00:07:21,380
Lots of people tried in America.

85
00:07:21,381 --> 00:07:27,380
There were lots of plans of towers, but they never built them.

86
00:07:27,381 --> 00:07:29,380
A competition was held for design.

87
00:07:29,381 --> 00:07:34,380
Architects from all over France submitted elaborate plans.

88
00:07:34,381 --> 00:07:40,380
The first one was done by a man called Maurice Coquin.

89
00:07:40,381 --> 00:07:45,000
He was one of the first engineers in Gustave Eiffel's staff.

90
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:49,800
Gustave Eiffel was France's premier architect-engineer.

91
00:07:50,340 --> 00:07:53,260
His reputation sprang from his visionary work on bridges.

92
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:58,420
He explored the revolutionary concept of metal frame construction.

93
00:08:00,220 --> 00:08:04,340
His genius attracted the finest architects in France to his studio.

94
00:08:04,341 --> 00:08:10,620
and Eiffel's association with the project virtually guaranteed its funding and completion.

95
00:08:12,760 --> 00:08:18,420
Two young engineers on Eiffel's staff, Maurice Cochlin and Emile Nogier,

96
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:25,280
actually created the concept of the 300-meter tower for the 1890 exhibition in Paris.

97
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:29,140
They sketched the first crude designs for a framed structure,

98
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:33,720
then called on architect Stephen Savastra to add ornamental flourishes.

99
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:42,260
the arching floral elements and sculptural nuances their romantic inspiration was an 1887

100
00:08:42,261 --> 00:08:47,780
french architectural notion that one could touch the sky from a structure if it reached the

101
00:08:47,781 --> 00:08:55,860
impossible height of 1000 feet if fell saw their plans and shared their dream their work was soon

102
00:08:55,861 --> 00:09:02,500
adopted by monsieur ethelle at first he agreed to add his name to the contest submission to improve

103
00:09:02,501 --> 00:09:07,140
its chances of winning but he quickly became interested in the tower concept

104
00:09:07,141 --> 00:09:13,060
and began to add his own unique flourishes once if hell's name was on the

105
00:09:13,061 --> 00:09:18,060
project everyone knew the outcome of the competition with his social and

106
00:09:18,061 --> 00:09:22,220
political connections if fell was well equipped to push the project through the

107
00:09:22,221 --> 00:09:26,320
Parisian bureaucracy and he had the technical ability to transform the

108
00:09:26,321 --> 00:09:31,960
project from a paper design to a three-dimensional reality the tower was

109
00:09:31,961 --> 00:09:36,772
designed to represent France's participation in the industrial age.

110
00:09:36,773 --> 00:09:38,280
As a symbol and as an

111
00:09:38,281 --> 00:09:43,475
engineering challenge, there was a lot riding on the success of the tower.

112
00:09:43,476 --> 00:09:45,160
But Ethel was determined

113
00:09:45,161 --> 00:09:50,920
to exhibit the same innovative spirit in construction that he had employed in the design.

114
00:09:50,921 --> 00:10:04,921
it is air and light form and motion as sturdy as man's resolve and as ethereal as his thoughts

115
00:10:09,220 --> 00:10:18,520
the Eiffel Tower is as much an idea of structure as it is an extraordinary physical monument and

116
00:10:18,521 --> 00:10:24,380
And the process of its construction reflects both the concept of prefabrication from the industrial age

117
00:10:24,381 --> 00:10:28,040
and the advanced building theories of the dawning 20th century.

118
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:33,040
February 28, 1887.

119
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:38,900
Gustave Ethel gathered a crowd of dignitaries to witness the commencement of construction.

120
00:10:40,180 --> 00:10:44,780
He was 53, and this tower was to be his crowning achievement.

121
00:10:44,781 --> 00:10:55,280
As the ceremonies proceeded, 50 engineers were still drafting over 5,300 detailed blueprints

122
00:10:55,281 --> 00:10:58,220
for the 132 workmen at the site.

123
00:10:59,380 --> 00:11:02,780
It would take four months to lay the foundation for the legs.

124
00:11:03,220 --> 00:11:08,880
Two pillars were set on 6 1⁄2-foot-thick concrete slabs set 23 feet below ground.

125
00:11:09,580 --> 00:11:13,260
The remaining two legs were positioned so close to the Seine River

126
00:11:13,261 --> 00:11:17,340
that watertight metal dams had to be lowered into the damp earth

127
00:11:17,341 --> 00:11:21,140
so the concrete could set beneath the seeping water line.

128
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:27,580
The square encompassed by the four legs was 426 feet on each side,

129
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:33,400
broad enough to distribute evenly the structure's 7,000 tons of iron.

130
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,820
Onto these foundations arose masonry bases

131
00:11:37,821 --> 00:11:41,120
embedded with two anchoring bolts for each of the four feet.

132
00:11:41,121 --> 00:11:47,420
From here, the legs would rise at a sharp 60-degree angle as hollow framework beams.

133
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:56,580
The beams were made of angle brackets and flat bars riveted together with stiffeners attached to the sides.

134
00:11:57,480 --> 00:12:00,420
Four of these assemblies combined to form the entire beam.

135
00:12:01,020 --> 00:12:05,360
The beam had the same structural dynamic as a series of interlocking cubes.

136
00:12:05,361 --> 00:12:14,660
and this is the genius of a fells design the framework support is as sturdy as solid stone

137
00:12:14,661 --> 00:12:22,100
at only a fraction of the weight it was also easily erected with standardized inexpensive

138
00:12:22,101 --> 00:12:32,900
prefabricated materials eventually 18,000 metal parts and 2 million 500,000 rivets would comprise

139
00:12:32,901 --> 00:12:38,920
the tower. Yet all of the pieces are either flat bars, angle brackets, or plates.

140
00:12:42,060 --> 00:12:46,103
The first stage of the construction was also the most critical.

141
00:12:46,104 --> 00:12:47,900
All four legs needed to rise

142
00:12:47,901 --> 00:12:54,054
simultaneously and meet on an exact horizontal plane at the tower's first floor.

143
00:12:54,055 --> 00:12:54,900
The radical

144
00:12:54,901 --> 00:13:00,160
angle demanded scaffolding support for each of the legs and a center scaffold to support the

145
00:13:00,161 --> 00:13:04,760
center ring girder which held the structure together.

146
00:13:04,761 --> 00:13:08,660
800-ton thrust jacks operated by manual hydraulic pumps

147
00:13:08,661 --> 00:13:11,920
installed under each leg helped to raise and lower

148
00:13:11,921 --> 00:13:14,860
the four sides independently in order

149
00:13:14,861 --> 00:13:18,460
to reach the required precision.

150
00:13:18,461 --> 00:13:20,560
When this was achieved, Gustave Eiffel

151
00:13:20,561 --> 00:13:22,600
knew there was nothing that could stop

152
00:13:22,601 --> 00:13:26,560
the completion of his dream, although there were those who

153
00:13:26,561 --> 00:13:28,000
would try.

154
00:13:28,001 --> 00:13:33,120
Eiffel was annoyed by people who lived near the tower, who said that it would fall on their houses.

155
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:37,720
And nobody would cover the risk. He covered it by himself.

156
00:13:39,160 --> 00:13:43,720
Likewise, many people feared that the tower would destroy the Paris skyline.

157
00:13:43,721 --> 00:13:50,200
When it was started to be constructed, some people tried to stop it, you see.

158
00:13:50,820 --> 00:13:56,480
And some painters, writers, architects wrote a letter saying it's a scandal.

159
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:04,080
The Paris arts community saw the tower as a rude industrial imposition on the city's beauty.

160
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:10,020
Dozens of French writers and painters attacked the tower in a public declaration.

161
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:18,820
I feel Paris is threatened, claimed one artist, by this positively tragic lampstand issuing from her stomach.

162
00:14:21,620 --> 00:14:23,820
Eiffel responded with equal passion.

163
00:14:23,821 --> 00:14:30,300
the tower will be the highest building ever raised by man he exclaimed will it

164
00:14:30,301 --> 00:14:34,520
not have a majesty of its own

165
00:14:41,540 --> 00:14:49,020
by April 1st 1888 the 300-meter tower of Paris was an eruption of iron lifting

166
00:14:49,021 --> 00:14:54,020
skyward and carrying with it the spirit of France.

167
00:14:54,021 --> 00:14:56,620
And finally, the general public, it seemed,

168
00:14:56,621 --> 00:14:59,760
was falling in love.

169
00:14:59,761 --> 00:15:02,600
The people in Paris, the general public,

170
00:15:02,601 --> 00:15:06,300
really felt a very strong feeling of admiration

171
00:15:06,301 --> 00:15:07,300
toward this tower.

172
00:15:07,301 --> 00:15:10,100
What had begun as the skeletal suggestion of form

173
00:15:10,101 --> 00:15:13,920
was now exhibiting grand flowing design,

174
00:15:13,921 --> 00:15:18,080
the graceful rise of the legs, the arcing, decorative

175
00:15:18,081 --> 00:15:22,680
additions contrasting with the stern regiment of exposed bolts, here was a

176
00:15:22,681 --> 00:15:29,320
work of art that spoke to an aggressive world preparing for dramatic change.

177
00:15:29,321 --> 00:15:35,580
The work progressed with lightning speed. Eiffel was mounting the operation as if

178
00:15:35,581 --> 00:15:39,580
he were conducting a war with crisp military precision.

179
00:15:39,581 --> 00:15:47,180
The structure was in fact prefabricated in the Eiffel factory. While the tower was

180
00:15:47,181 --> 00:15:52,760
assembled and the most you had something like 300 people working on the site.

181
00:15:52,761 --> 00:15:57,080
Bits of the structure littered the site like scattered pieces of a child's

182
00:15:57,081 --> 00:16:02,300
erector set. Riveting teams worked in groups of four to keep up with the

183
00:16:02,301 --> 00:16:08,120
frantic pace. As soon as one load of iron was attached, four 12-ton cranes

184
00:16:08,121 --> 00:16:13,640
would deliver more, crawling up the guide shafts where future elevators would soar.

185
00:16:13,641 --> 00:16:18,140
The second floor was finished a little over a year after the start of construction,

186
00:16:18,141 --> 00:16:25,840
and less than a year after that, on March 31st, 1889, the entire tower was complete.

187
00:16:25,841 --> 00:16:33,560
It had taken only two years, two months, and five days to build what would be the

188
00:16:33,561 --> 00:16:39,440
world's tallest structure for the next 40 years. Remarkably, there was not a single

189
00:16:39,441 --> 00:16:44,040
death as the result of the challenging and dangerous work.

190
00:16:44,041 --> 00:16:50,180
Eiffel himself was the first to climb the 1710 steps to the summit and unfurl the French

191
00:16:50,181 --> 00:16:52,680
tricolor flag.

192
00:16:52,681 --> 00:16:55,520
Beneath him, the city of Paris.

193
00:16:55,521 --> 00:17:00,560
It seemed as if its soul was being funneled up the mighty legs of the tower and sprayed

194
00:17:00,561 --> 00:17:06,320
across the sky in a celestial celebration.

195
00:17:06,321 --> 00:17:11,240
When the 1889 Universal Exposition of Paris was officially opened, the glittering centerpiece

196
00:17:11,241 --> 00:17:14,640
was the 300-meter tower.

197
00:17:14,641 --> 00:17:19,860
Officials called it the greatest crowd-flabbergasting machine ever built.

198
00:17:19,861 --> 00:17:24,200
But soon it was simply to be known as the Eiffel Tower.

199
00:17:24,201 --> 00:17:38,201
The tower was called at first the tower of 320 meters, even in the beginning the pylon

200
00:17:43,520 --> 00:17:48,080
of 320 meters, then the 320 meter tower.

201
00:17:48,081 --> 00:17:51,732
I don't know exactly when it became the Tower of Mr.

202
00:17:51,733 --> 00:17:54,120
Eiffel and later the Eiffel Tower.

203
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:04,920
I think it escaped him that it would go any further than a very high functional tower of great dimension.

204
00:18:09,680 --> 00:18:13,860
The Eiffel Tower is rooted in both the majestic and the mundane.

205
00:18:13,861 --> 00:18:16,860
It may have been the tallest structure on the planet,

206
00:18:16,861 --> 00:18:19,860
but it was equally cherished by Monsieur Eiffel

207
00:18:19,861 --> 00:18:23,860
because of the tremendous personal income it generated.

208
00:18:23,861 --> 00:18:28,860
You know, Mr. Eiffel practically funded the construction of the tower.

209
00:18:28,861 --> 00:18:32,860
In fact, he paid for most of it, and he made a deal.

210
00:18:32,861 --> 00:18:35,860
He said, if you give me the right to work on it for 20 years,

211
00:18:35,861 --> 00:18:37,860
I'll agree to finance it 80%.

212
00:18:37,861 --> 00:18:40,860
If he had not believed in the success of his tower,

213
00:18:40,861 --> 00:18:42,860
he would not have jumped into this venture

214
00:18:42,861 --> 00:18:47,860
this venture in which he succeeded so magnificently.

215
00:18:47,861 --> 00:18:52,640
Monsieur Eiffel had a private apartment on the tower from which he conducted scientific

216
00:18:52,641 --> 00:18:56,740
experiments and met with important dignitaries.

217
00:18:56,741 --> 00:19:01,200
All the while he could watch the elevators lifting nearly two million tourists to the

218
00:19:01,201 --> 00:19:08,420
top in the first year, representing over one million dollars income to Eiffel as he personally

219
00:19:08,421 --> 00:19:10,800
garnered the receipts from ticket sales.

220
00:19:10,801 --> 00:19:17,120
Eiffel could earn money with the people going up and he made a great lot of money.

221
00:19:17,960 --> 00:19:18,900
He was very rich after.

222
00:19:19,900 --> 00:19:22,420
Eiffel was not at all an artist.

223
00:19:23,040 --> 00:19:24,160
He was an entrepreneur.

224
00:19:26,220 --> 00:19:32,280
Contrasting opinions of Monsieur Eiffel spring from the multifaceted and conflicting nature of his character.

225
00:19:33,900 --> 00:19:40,140
He may not have been an artist, but his approach to metal frame construction established a structural aesthetic

226
00:19:40,141 --> 00:19:42,180
which is still admired today.

227
00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:49,460
He may have been a national hero after building the tower in 1889,

228
00:19:49,840 --> 00:19:52,140
but the next year, he was convicted of profiteering

229
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:56,000
in the failed French effort to construct a Panama Canal.

230
00:19:57,480 --> 00:20:01,620
The canal was the dream of French engineer Ferdinand de Lesseps,

231
00:20:01,720 --> 00:20:03,740
who had triumphed with his Suez Canal

232
00:20:03,741 --> 00:20:06,800
and was determined to duplicate that feat in Panama.

233
00:20:06,801 --> 00:20:11,840
De Lessepte invited Eiffel to suggest an engineering approach to accomplish the

234
00:20:11,841 --> 00:20:17,900
jungle waterway and Eiffel proposed an innovative system of locks. That idea was

235
00:20:17,901 --> 00:20:25,560
rejected by De Lessepte and the result was disastrous. Canals cannot be carved

236
00:20:25,561 --> 00:20:30,620
through swamps and jungle. The attempt proved so expensive and unproductive the

237
00:20:30,621 --> 00:20:35,920
French government was nearly thrown into bankruptcy. The political fallout was

238
00:20:35,921 --> 00:20:40,120
severe anyone even remotely associated with the failed attempt was vilified and

239
00:20:40,121 --> 00:20:45,880
if fell was sentenced to 24 months in prison that judgment was later

240
00:20:45,881 --> 00:20:50,240
overturned but by then if fell had lost his appetite for the construction

241
00:20:50,241 --> 00:20:57,080
business was considered by sir by all engineers like like a god you see and he

242
00:20:57,081 --> 00:21:03,040
was very he was he was also a man of science he loved science and when after

243
00:21:03,041 --> 00:21:06,702
the tower he stopped building things.

244
00:21:06,703 --> 00:21:12,740
Instead, Yeffel, now 73, dedicated himself to pure scientific

245
00:21:12,741 --> 00:21:17,150
study, specifically the exploration of aerodynamics.

246
00:21:17,151 --> 00:21:21,220
Other French visionaries like author Jules Verne

247
00:21:21,221 --> 00:21:27,940
had applied crude 19th century notions to the concept of flight as illustrated in this early

248
00:21:27,941 --> 00:21:30,720
film proposing a trip to the moon.

249
00:21:32,460 --> 00:21:37,100
Ethel realized that advancements in aviation would need to be based on the scientific study

250
00:21:37,101 --> 00:21:38,360
of the natural elements.

251
00:21:39,260 --> 00:21:44,160
But while some well-meaning researchers were mired in the idea of outlandish mechanical

252
00:21:44,161 --> 00:21:49,300
contraptions, and others were attaching feathered wings to their arms and tossing themselves

253
00:21:49,301 --> 00:21:55,160
off the tower like birds, Ethel returned to his analytical roots and built a workshop

254
00:21:55,161 --> 00:21:57,340
that still operates today.

255
00:21:57,940 --> 00:21:58,640
Mr.

256
00:21:58,641 --> 00:22:08,180
Eiffel, well, he made the first wind resistance tests, because all of his life he had problems

257
00:22:08,181 --> 00:22:12,717
with the wind. That was his enemy, number one.

258
00:22:12,718 --> 00:22:16,760
And in 1906 to 1909, he began to question

259
00:22:16,761 --> 00:22:21,416
aerodynamic resistance, and that's why he made this first wind tunnel.

260
00:22:21,417 --> 00:22:22,480
One can say that

261
00:22:22,481 --> 00:22:24,880
in this wind tunnel where you're standing right now,

262
00:22:25,580 --> 00:22:27,800
French aviationary studies began.

263
00:22:31,640 --> 00:22:36,220
I must specify that the tunnel, the ventilator, the motor,

264
00:22:36,640 --> 00:22:38,280
and all of these are originals.

265
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:39,200
We don't touch them.

266
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:41,440
We leave them in their original state.

267
00:22:42,380 --> 00:22:44,860
As for measuring devices, of course,

268
00:22:44,861 --> 00:22:46,980
we adapted them for modern functions.

269
00:22:46,981 --> 00:22:54,080
Mr. Eiffel was an extraordinary engineer.

270
00:22:54,800 --> 00:22:57,040
We've examined all he did during his lifetime

271
00:22:57,041 --> 00:22:58,520
and all that he envisioned.

272
00:22:59,300 --> 00:23:04,440
And today, we are doing things that Mr. Eiffel already did in 1917.

273
00:23:08,660 --> 00:23:11,840
Eiffel's biggest vision, the 300-meter tower,

274
00:23:12,040 --> 00:23:14,980
was only supposed to stand for 20 years.

275
00:23:14,981 --> 00:23:21,060
But he had grown accustomed to using the structure as his personal reception area,

276
00:23:21,520 --> 00:23:25,060
a way to maintain his high profile over Parisian society.

277
00:23:26,580 --> 00:23:32,340
So he launched an aggressive campaign to save his tower from the wrecking ball.

278
00:23:34,160 --> 00:23:38,720
He conducted over 5,000 experiments in his lofty laboratory,

279
00:23:38,721 --> 00:23:44,180
but it was radio that saved the day and the tower.

280
00:23:44,980 --> 00:23:48,640
The giant metal structure was the perfect antenna.

281
00:23:49,540 --> 00:23:53,400
From the top, signals could be transmitted as far away as North America.

282
00:23:54,400 --> 00:23:57,520
So although destined for demolition in 1909,

283
00:23:58,560 --> 00:24:05,760
Eiffel's tower won a reprieve as radio helped France monitor the dark clouds of war looming over Germany.

284
00:24:07,340 --> 00:24:11,420
In 1914, World War swept over the European continent.

285
00:24:11,421 --> 00:24:18,760
For Paris, the conflict came so close that soldiers often took taxis to travel to the front lines.

286
00:24:20,200 --> 00:24:25,880
But with the help of the Eiffel Tower, generals could listen in on the movements of the enemy

287
00:24:25,881 --> 00:24:29,400
and declare zeppelin alerts from their elevated perch.

288
00:24:32,720 --> 00:24:38,680
In 1923, five years after the last echoes of the war, Gustave Eiffel died.

289
00:24:38,681 --> 00:24:44,740
throughout his 91 years his vision propelled France to the forefront of

290
00:24:44,741 --> 00:24:51,280
emerging technologies his interpretation of design and structure as represented

291
00:24:51,281 --> 00:24:55,600
by his tower was the world's introduction to basic 20th century

292
00:24:55,601 --> 00:25:01,440
principles the future of construction would forevermore be linked to the

293
00:25:01,441 --> 00:25:06,440
a unified participation of modest interlocking elements.

294
00:25:14,440 --> 00:25:17,440
It was a concept whose time had come.

295
00:25:17,441 --> 00:25:30,520
After the devastation of World War,

296
00:25:30,700 --> 00:25:33,140
Paris was anxious to lift its battered spirits

297
00:25:33,141 --> 00:25:34,920
and embrace a brighter day.

298
00:25:36,320 --> 00:25:40,060
Yet some sensed a subtle, desperate undercurrent

299
00:25:40,061 --> 00:25:41,840
to the celebratory esprit.

300
00:25:42,480 --> 00:25:45,380
It was as if the frantic good times were masking

301
00:25:45,381 --> 00:25:49,140
the many issues the war had left unsettled.

302
00:25:51,400 --> 00:25:55,200
Paris in the 20s attracted a lost generation,

303
00:25:56,200 --> 00:25:59,520
disillusioned with traditional values and philosophies.

304
00:26:00,560 --> 00:26:05,580
Some of these post-war Parisians were actually American artists and intellectuals

305
00:26:05,581 --> 00:26:09,260
searching for meaning in a seemingly chaotic world.

306
00:26:11,200 --> 00:26:14,320
F. Scott Fitzgerald and his wife Zelda.

307
00:26:15,380 --> 00:26:21,340
Charlie Chaplin, movie stars, painters, and a mixed assortment of creative individuals

308
00:26:21,341 --> 00:26:26,360
became attracted to a French revolution in thinking called existentialism.

309
00:26:27,440 --> 00:26:34,520
Is what we are the sum of our existence, our time and poetry, nothing more than absurdist fantasy?

310
00:26:36,080 --> 00:26:39,240
Darker themes of alienation started to emerge in the arts,

311
00:26:39,241 --> 00:26:46,360
and ironically, the tower provided a visual and symbolic link to a lighter and more stable time.

312
00:26:47,720 --> 00:26:53,660
After all, Gustave Eiffel had created weightless beauty from dense, heavy metal,

313
00:26:54,380 --> 00:26:56,400
a very reassuring concept.

314
00:26:58,140 --> 00:27:04,140
The Eiffel Tower is not only the iron composing it, but also what's inside, air,

315
00:27:05,340 --> 00:27:07,620
captured in a latticework of metal.

316
00:27:07,621 --> 00:27:12,620
Some visitors feel that they are in the middle of nowhere.

317
00:27:12,621 --> 00:27:17,620
The sensation suggests that it's not only the structure, but also its void,

318
00:27:17,621 --> 00:27:22,620
which is the essential material of the Eiffel Tower.

319
00:27:22,621 --> 00:27:29,620
The tower's bold composition seemed a poetic link between the 19th and 20th centuries.

320
00:27:29,621 --> 00:27:39,620
A link which would be brutally severed when fascism, racism and systematic murder erupted

321
00:27:39,621 --> 00:27:49,960
from the ashes of Germany's defeat in the First World War.

322
00:27:49,961 --> 00:27:54,960
In May 1940, in the terrifying panic of a Nazi blitzkrieg,

323
00:27:54,961 --> 00:27:58,960
Paris and the Eiffel Tower were taken hostage.

324
00:28:01,960 --> 00:28:05,960
But German troops found it difficult fully to utilize the monument.

325
00:28:05,961 --> 00:28:08,960
Frenchmen had damaged the elevators.

326
00:28:08,961 --> 00:28:13,960
This sabotage kept even Hitler from savoring his conquest.

327
00:28:13,961 --> 00:28:17,960
There's a very famous photo.

328
00:28:17,961 --> 00:28:20,840
He's on the Trocadero, he looks at the tower, but he hasn't been on it.

329
00:28:21,180 --> 00:28:22,900
There were no lifts, couldn't go.

330
00:28:24,200 --> 00:28:28,640
Seeing the tower occupied by Nazis enraged French citizens.

331
00:28:29,520 --> 00:28:32,420
Parisians joined together in futile counterattacks.

332
00:28:33,300 --> 00:28:37,980
The free French secretly conspired in the ancient Roman catacombs,

333
00:28:37,981 --> 00:28:40,220
which weaved beneath the streets of Paris.

334
00:28:41,160 --> 00:28:44,460
Tunnels hiding the bones of forgotten and vanquished Frenchmen

335
00:28:44,461 --> 00:28:48,620
throughout the centuries were now home to the resistance.

336
00:28:50,220 --> 00:28:54,600
There are subterranean passages that go underneath the French capital.

337
00:28:55,880 --> 00:28:58,740
The catacombs played an important role in the resistance.

338
00:28:59,400 --> 00:29:03,540
It was a means for us to travel across Paris without being seen

339
00:29:03,541 --> 00:29:07,460
in order to be able to lead the Parisian insurrection on the offense.

340
00:29:07,461 --> 00:29:17,600
We derailed trains because those trains that went to the front lines of the Atlantic all went behind Paris.

341
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:24,240
I participated in the sabotage of railroad tracks,

342
00:29:24,900 --> 00:29:28,500
tracks that were obviously meant for trains that transported soldiers.

343
00:29:30,820 --> 00:29:35,720
But one day, we found out about a large train that circled Paris.

344
00:29:37,460 --> 00:29:45,980
We made arrangements, we derailed the train, and it was just awful, because the train was packed with sauerkraut.

345
00:29:46,620 --> 00:29:50,020
There were particles of sauerkraut for miles around.

346
00:29:52,980 --> 00:29:59,300
Hitler despised the free French and planned to destroy their most treasured monument out of spite.

347
00:30:00,100 --> 00:30:04,260
At the war's end, Hitler gave orders to have the tower bombed.

348
00:30:04,261 --> 00:30:13,100
But General von Cholitz, who had fallen in love with the city of Paris, gave the Eiffel Tower an unauthorized reprieve.

349
00:30:19,440 --> 00:30:28,100
People of Western Europe, a landing was made this morning on the coast of France by troops of the Allied Expeditionary Force.

350
00:30:29,180 --> 00:30:31,480
The hour of your liberation is approaching.

351
00:30:34,260 --> 00:30:41,260
American tanks finally drove the Nazis from the Eiffel Tower.

352
00:30:53,260 --> 00:30:58,260
But when liberation finally came to Paris, the French government was in a shambles.

353
00:30:58,261 --> 00:31:01,260
The old leaders were gone.

354
00:31:01,261 --> 00:31:06,140
The next generation was ill-equipped to raise the country to its former glory.

355
00:31:07,680 --> 00:31:14,500
From 1945 through 1960, the people of France voted 20 different provisional governments into power.

356
00:31:15,260 --> 00:31:16,720
It was political chaos.

357
00:31:17,560 --> 00:31:24,420
Until General Charles de Gaulle was finally persuaded to lead the country out of post-war calamity.

358
00:31:25,340 --> 00:31:29,060
Only a man of his strength and broad appeal could have saved the republic.

359
00:31:29,061 --> 00:31:35,060
De Gaulle rallied the disheartened country and gave France back its spirit.

360
00:31:35,061 --> 00:31:42,060
Its soul, however, had always been protected in that giant cage of the Eiffel Tower.

361
00:31:42,061 --> 00:31:47,060
But just as France needed a new vision for the second half of the 20th century,

362
00:31:47,061 --> 00:31:51,060
the Eiffel Tower needed a new commitment to its care and maintenance,

363
00:31:51,061 --> 00:31:54,060
if it were to stand at all.

364
00:31:59,060 --> 00:32:13,060
It was designed as the centerpiece of an international exposition in 1889.

365
00:32:19,180 --> 00:32:23,760
Today, more than a century later, the celebration continues,

366
00:32:24,180 --> 00:32:28,580
as well over a thousand people visit the Eiffel Tower every hour.

367
00:32:29,060 --> 00:32:43,060
There are four elevators to carry them to the top, one in each of the tower legs rising

368
00:32:45,940 --> 00:32:50,680
at a steep 60 degree angle.

369
00:32:50,681 --> 00:32:55,680
The heart of the hoisting is actually hidden beneath the legs in a century-old catacomb.

370
00:32:59,680 --> 00:33:05,680
Here, complex contraptions rivaled Jules Verne's most elaborate fantasies.

371
00:33:05,681 --> 00:33:10,680
The original principle was to have water under pressure, pushing a piston.

372
00:33:10,681 --> 00:33:17,680
There was pushing a dolly. The dolly was pulling the cable to heave the car up.

373
00:33:17,681 --> 00:33:25,880
The 100-year-old technology forms an animated mosaic, giving the impression that the lifting

374
00:33:25,881 --> 00:33:29,280
is done by a giant mechanical toy.

375
00:33:29,281 --> 00:33:35,440
This piston rings which are inside here, there's square cotton wreath about that big,

376
00:33:35,441 --> 00:33:39,440
which you press in, and they're greased with mutton fat.

377
00:33:39,441 --> 00:33:40,440
See what?

378
00:33:40,441 --> 00:33:41,440
See?

379
00:33:41,441 --> 00:33:43,920
Very white, very nice one.

380
00:33:43,921 --> 00:33:46,480
twice a week here, it smells like having a Mechoui,

381
00:33:46,481 --> 00:33:49,960
you know, this North African roasted lamb.

382
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:51,840
That's it.

383
00:33:53,280 --> 00:33:55,200
All technology works beautifully.

384
00:33:57,440 --> 00:33:59,280
That's leather.

385
00:34:01,720 --> 00:34:03,960
That's leather, same material.

386
00:34:03,961 --> 00:34:05,640
But it's compressed, it's shaped,

387
00:34:05,641 --> 00:34:07,440
you have various shapes.

388
00:34:07,441 --> 00:34:10,440
It's true leather and it's impregnated

389
00:34:10,441 --> 00:34:13,000
with oil which is made from the hooves of oxen.

390
00:34:13,920 --> 00:34:23,920
In 1980, officials decided to upgrade the system by blending the old technology with

391
00:34:23,921 --> 00:34:25,920
the latest high-tech equipment.

392
00:34:25,921 --> 00:34:30,920
It's like mixing the engine of a Ferrari and the engine of a Mack truck.

393
00:34:30,921 --> 00:34:36,920
At the third level, a second bank of elevators carries passengers vertically to the summit.

394
00:34:36,921 --> 00:34:41,920
Atop the structure, maintenance is non-stop.

395
00:34:41,921 --> 00:34:51,280
Every day a small but determined team of 25 workers walk the narrow iron supports for the 18-month job of painting the tower.

396
00:34:52,040 --> 00:34:55,960
The process is the same today as when the tower was originally painted.

397
00:34:57,040 --> 00:35:04,420
Over 50 tons of paint is required to complete the task, a task that is repeated every seven years.

398
00:35:07,020 --> 00:35:11,820
I was 18 years old when I began this job in the air as an industrial painter.

399
00:35:11,920 --> 00:35:21,040
To work on the Eiffel Tower, such an important monument, I feel privileged and happy.

400
00:35:24,960 --> 00:35:28,200
But being a painter is somewhat specialized.

401
00:35:28,201 --> 00:35:32,620
You have to be physically strong and have a good morale.

402
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:34,180
It's dangerous.

403
00:35:34,860 --> 00:35:37,760
One requires strength and balance, of course.

404
00:35:41,920 --> 00:35:46,240
The higher I am, the better I feel.

405
00:35:47,140 --> 00:35:50,200
But painters aren't alone on the elevated web of iron.

406
00:35:50,860 --> 00:35:52,960
Electricians also comb the tower.

407
00:35:55,480 --> 00:35:58,160
Personally, I'd much rather work up here than in an office.

408
00:35:58,800 --> 00:36:00,120
I think my friend agrees.

409
00:36:01,500 --> 00:36:03,800
Absolutely. I mean, we get to be outdoors.

410
00:36:04,240 --> 00:36:05,040
We like what we do.

411
00:36:05,900 --> 00:36:09,720
To be able to work on a monument like the Eiffel Tower is a privilege, I'd say.

412
00:36:11,920 --> 00:36:16,860
The exposed nature of the structure occasionally allows the elements to inflict damage.

413
00:36:17,720 --> 00:36:22,360
Water pipes can freeze and burst if not protected by the electrical system.

414
00:36:23,420 --> 00:36:25,880
There are heating bands with thermostats.

415
00:36:27,080 --> 00:36:31,040
As soon as the temperature falls below 40 degrees, the heating bands kick in.

416
00:36:32,140 --> 00:36:33,240
The piping is heated.

417
00:36:35,680 --> 00:36:40,120
But the main job of the electricians is to maintain the illuminated beauty.

418
00:36:40,121 --> 00:36:44,920
360 light bulbs are constantly inspected and changed.

419
00:36:45,540 --> 00:36:51,120
But climbing to the sockets often upsets the real residents of the tower, the birds.

420
00:36:52,660 --> 00:36:54,100
They're attracted by the light.

421
00:36:54,680 --> 00:36:56,640
Once they come close, they get a bit lost.

422
00:36:57,320 --> 00:36:59,900
When they try to sit out again, they get caught in the steelwork.

423
00:37:00,260 --> 00:37:02,720
But a lot of them manage to fly off unharmed.

424
00:37:04,220 --> 00:37:07,460
We've actually even seen birds come and make their nests in the steel beams.

425
00:37:08,100 --> 00:37:10,980
It's kind of nice because in Paris we're not used to seeing pigeons anymore.

426
00:37:11,980 --> 00:37:12,920
It's a pleasant change.

427
00:37:18,120 --> 00:37:23,720
Early evening, the sun casts its last golden pink rays over the city of Paris,

428
00:37:24,480 --> 00:37:28,360
the homes, the museums, the monuments and cathedrals,

429
00:37:28,361 --> 00:37:31,780
and the couples who gather on the tower.

430
00:37:31,781 --> 00:37:39,980
The tower responds as if its own glorious wash of color is the reflected glow of lovers.

431
00:37:41,280 --> 00:37:45,460
The lighting design is calculated to enhance the romantic ambiance.

432
00:37:50,060 --> 00:37:55,000
The paint on the Eiffel Tower contains a yellow pigment.

433
00:37:56,900 --> 00:38:00,420
Lighting it with yellow light brings out the yellow pigment.

434
00:38:00,421 --> 00:38:07,420
That's been the major source of success in this operation.

435
00:38:07,421 --> 00:38:11,420
The approach is entirely different

436
00:38:11,421 --> 00:38:16,420
because night lighting must interpret the feeling,

437
00:38:16,421 --> 00:38:20,420
even the expression of the monument.

438
00:38:20,421 --> 00:38:28,420
The Eiffel Tower lighting seeks to validate the architecture

439
00:38:28,421 --> 00:38:31,420
of this star attraction in Paris.

440
00:38:32,920 --> 00:38:35,920
It's the star of Paris at night.

441
00:38:49,180 --> 00:38:52,920
People who work at the Tau are very proud.

442
00:38:52,921 --> 00:38:54,920
Some even too proud.

443
00:38:54,921 --> 00:38:57,420
It's like if they had built the Tau.

444
00:38:58,420 --> 00:39:02,500
There is a sense of ownership which is shared among those who work the tower,

445
00:39:03,280 --> 00:39:06,420
especially the employees whose task is to greet visitors.

446
00:39:09,460 --> 00:39:12,420
My job, in fact, is to organize the tour guides.

447
00:39:12,900 --> 00:39:14,320
I am responsible for the group.

448
00:39:16,220 --> 00:39:17,880
We're now in the north pillar.

449
00:39:18,480 --> 00:39:22,080
Each of the four corners of the Eiffel Tower is represented by pillars

450
00:39:22,081 --> 00:39:24,040
in which you will find the elevators.

451
00:39:24,660 --> 00:39:26,460
Down there is the beautiful France restaurant

452
00:39:26,461 --> 00:39:30,220
and above it the Parisian restaurant, the two restaurants of the first level.

453
00:39:33,160 --> 00:39:37,600
And over there is the Cinemax Gallery where you'll find a post office,

454
00:39:38,120 --> 00:39:40,820
souvenir shops, buffets, ice cream vendors.

455
00:39:41,160 --> 00:39:43,240
There are lots of interesting things in the gallery.

456
00:39:45,900 --> 00:39:49,060
The tower was originally designed as a tourist mecca

457
00:39:49,061 --> 00:39:52,140
and it still serves that lighthearted function today.

458
00:39:53,100 --> 00:39:56,200
But its magnificent vistas and staggering height

459
00:39:56,201 --> 00:39:59,220
sometimes attract a darker element.

460
00:40:01,560 --> 00:40:05,000
People periodically try to jump off the Eiffel Tower.

461
00:40:06,000 --> 00:40:08,460
Already 380 people have committed suicide.

462
00:40:09,120 --> 00:40:11,440
That's an average of about three or four a year,

463
00:40:11,880 --> 00:40:14,920
so we regularly try to control the situation.

464
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:20,840
We try to reason with them.

465
00:40:21,500 --> 00:40:23,140
We try to find out their motive.

466
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:25,040
We discuss it with them.

467
00:40:25,041 --> 00:40:29,480
we try to talk with them as much as possible until security comes and from

468
00:40:29,481 --> 00:40:36,400
then on it's the fireman's responsibility. More often a tour guide has the

469
00:40:36,401 --> 00:40:44,700
opportunity to see people in a more festive and romantic mood. I have seen a

470
00:40:44,701 --> 00:40:50,040
lot of couples walking arm-in-arm. They often come to celebrate an event or an

471
00:40:50,041 --> 00:40:51,423
anniversary.

472
00:40:51,424 --> 00:41:00,640
Our job also includes discouraging these people from riding on the Eiffel Tower.

473
00:41:01,320 --> 00:41:06,000
Most of the time we chase them and we try to catch them and sometimes it's a little game

474
00:41:06,001 --> 00:41:08,982
between the tourists and ourselves.

475
00:41:08,983 --> 00:41:14,180
We are on the third floor of the Eiffel Tower in front of the

476
00:41:14,181 --> 00:41:15,520
Jules Verne restaurant.

477
00:41:15,521 --> 00:41:19,540
This restaurant is one of the symbols of Paris precisely because it's

478
00:41:19,541 --> 00:41:21,944
located way up on the third floor.

479
00:41:21,945 --> 00:41:25,620
There's only one Eiffel Tower, only one third floor.

480
00:41:26,140 --> 00:41:30,020
It's certainly the best location in the world unless there's a restaurant on top of Mount Everest.

481
00:41:33,420 --> 00:41:39,340
The ceremony and celebration of haute cuisine is a tradition on the tower extending back to

482
00:41:39,341 --> 00:41:45,640
its earliest days. Food is more than nourishment for the French. It is a passion.

483
00:41:45,641 --> 00:41:49,323
It happened about six months after I arrived in Paris.

484
00:41:49,324 --> 00:41:52,120
I informed my wife that I had a mistress.

485
00:41:52,121 --> 00:41:55,850
She just stared at me and I said she's an old mistress.

486
00:41:55,851 --> 00:41:58,360
She looked even more surprised. After

487
00:41:58,361 --> 00:42:03,980
about five minutes I told her my mistress is the Eiffel Tower.

488
00:42:03,981 --> 00:42:06,880
Some artists paint, some work in

489
00:42:06,881 --> 00:42:07,581
stone.

490
00:42:07,582 --> 00:42:14,620
Master chef Alain Rex prefers pâté, practicing his culinary magic at the Jules Verne,

491
00:42:14,621 --> 00:42:18,940
the exclusive four-star restaurant midway up the lofty height of the tower.

492
00:42:18,941 --> 00:42:27,380
The view of Paris really inspires me. It inspires me to create new dishes every day.

493
00:42:27,381 --> 00:42:36,201
We have a hundred and fifteen people employed in the Jules Verne restaurant alone.

494
00:42:36,202 --> 00:42:37,600
There's a lot

495
00:42:37,601 --> 00:42:44,740
hustle and bustle a lot of people I love that armed with spatulas whisks

496
00:42:44,741 --> 00:42:49,300
paring knives and souffle dishes chef Rex and his men fight for the honor of

497
00:42:49,301 --> 00:43:03,301
France today's special a lobster salad and coral sauce it's French lobster the

498
00:43:04,080 --> 00:43:08,760
The tower was designed to inspire the common person to new visions.

499
00:43:08,761 --> 00:43:13,380
It also encourages the imaginations of artists to soar.

500
00:43:13,381 --> 00:43:18,920
At the Jules Verne restaurant, these two dynamics take flight.

501
00:43:18,921 --> 00:43:22,840
It is an appetizing blend that could only happen in the City of Light.

502
00:43:34,080 --> 00:43:45,480
Paris is often called the city of light.

503
00:43:47,360 --> 00:43:50,900
But to what quality of light does the title refer?

504
00:43:52,540 --> 00:43:55,580
Does it suggest the lightness of the French spirit?

505
00:43:56,120 --> 00:43:58,460
The enlightenment of the French culture?

506
00:43:59,020 --> 00:44:02,160
Or perhaps the brilliant light of the French sky?

507
00:44:02,161 --> 00:44:06,000
bleaching marble facades by day and by night,

508
00:44:06,001 --> 00:44:09,460
cradling lovers in its rose-colored evening mist.

509
00:44:11,300 --> 00:44:13,840
Maybe it's the magic light of a cabaret

510
00:44:13,841 --> 00:44:16,820
where Toulouse-Lautrec painted and played.

511
00:44:24,520 --> 00:44:27,320
With a glittering sparkle of a merry-go-round

512
00:44:27,321 --> 00:44:29,620
reflected in the eyes of the young at heart.

513
00:44:32,160 --> 00:44:35,980
The light of Paris is all these images and experiences,

514
00:44:36,700 --> 00:44:38,940
yet it finds its most vibrant voice

515
00:44:38,941 --> 00:44:43,220
in the silent exultation of Monsieur Eiffel's tower.

516
00:44:44,540 --> 00:44:47,060
I believe that the Eiffel Tower

517
00:44:47,061 --> 00:44:50,120
has taken an entirely unique place in the world.

518
00:44:50,660 --> 00:44:52,200
It's almost become a myth.

519
00:44:53,640 --> 00:44:56,340
It sort of glorifies science.

520
00:44:56,341 --> 00:44:58,560
It was built during an era

521
00:44:58,561 --> 00:45:01,100
when it was thought that science and technology

522
00:45:01,101 --> 00:45:02,680
would make mankind happy.

523
00:45:04,420 --> 00:45:07,080
I believe also because of its shape,

524
00:45:07,140 --> 00:45:10,260
it is very, very special and can be easily drawn.

525
00:45:11,940 --> 00:45:15,540
I think also that it is a useless object

526
00:45:15,541 --> 00:45:19,140
and that a useless object has a great therapeutic value.

527
00:45:21,280 --> 00:45:24,200
So the Eiffel Tower stands as a monument

528
00:45:24,201 --> 00:45:26,380
to those things we cannot grasp,

529
00:45:26,900 --> 00:45:28,880
our joys, our hopes.

530
00:45:28,881 --> 00:45:33,120
It still stands as a technological marvel,

531
00:45:35,180 --> 00:45:39,520
but in its simple, elegant, uplifting form,

532
00:45:39,940 --> 00:45:46,040
we find a way to celebrate life and love.

